<DocsLayout title="Authority and WorkCommissions in Haft v9" description="How v9 separates recommendations, decisions, plans, execution authority, performed work, and evidence.">
  <h1>Authority &amp; commissions</h1>

  <p>
    Haft keeps advice, binding choice, execution authority, performed work,
    and evidence in different records. Text generated by an agent can describe
    any of them. It cannot create operator authority by describing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Binding a decision</h2>

  <p>
    <code>h-decide</code> may route one direct, unambiguous operator request to
    a DecisionRecord effect. The request must identify:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>the exact effect;</li>
    <li>the readable subject;</li>
    <li>the selected option;</li>
    <li>the binding scope.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A manual <code>/h-decide</code> invocation is a compatible shortcut. It is
    not an approval receipt. Generated text, recommendations, quotations,
    pasted third-party requests, schemas, and model-supplied tool fields do not
    bind a decision.
  </p>

  <p>
    A supported host records <code>host_routed_operator_request</code> and uses
    the CLI/input-file effect sink. Direct MCP
    <code>haft_decision(action="decide")</code> remains fail-closed with
    <code>operator_confirmation_required</code> until a verifiable host receipt
    exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Human Gate Brief</h2>

  <p>
    When the operator's choice is missing or ambiguous, the agent stops only
    the affected operation and explains the gate in ordinary language. A valid
    brief includes:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>whether the gate is a bounded choice, authority grant, or SpecSection lifecycle act;</li>
    <li>the readable subject, affected operation, and why that operation is blocked;</li>
    <li>every real option, including defer or reject when available;</li>
    <li>what changes, what stays unchanged, the immediate consequence, and the weakest link of each option;</li>
    <li>the comparison basis and selection policy when they exist;</li>
    <li>the agent's advisory recommendation and evidence freshness.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The operator answers in natural language. Bare <code>yes</code> or
    <code>да</code> is usable only when one current brief already fixes one
    exact effect and selection. The brief itself is explanation, not
    authorization.
  </p>

  <h2>Plan, commission, work, and result</h2>

  <pre><code>DecisionRecord --may-authorize--> WorkCommission
WorkCommission --may-start--> RuntimeRunRecord
performed work --may-produce--> result and evidence</code></pre>

  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>WorkPlan</strong> describes intended actions and order.</li>
    <li>A <strong>WorkCommission</strong> grants bounded execution authority.</li>
    <li>A <strong>RuntimeRunRecord</strong> records one runner lifecycle.</li>
    <li>A result report records what the runner claims happened.</li>
    <li>Evidence supports or weakens a claim for a declared use.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    None of these substitutes for the next. A plan does not authorize work; a
    commission does not perform it; a passing result does not grant permission
    to apply, merge, push, deploy, or publish.
  </p>

  <h2>External runners in v9</h2>

  <p>
    v9 has no built-in coding-agent executor. Haft does not spawn an agent,
    create a worktree, apply a patch, merge, push, or publish. The host agent
    can perform already-authorized work directly, or an independently operated
    runner can consume the typed commission lifecycle.
  </p>

  <pre><code>haft commission create-from-decision dec-...
haft commission list-runnable
haft commission show wc-...
haft commission claim wc-... --runner external:my-runner
haft commission complete-external wc-... --runner external:my-runner \
  --verdict pass --payload-file evidence.json</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>h-commission</code> is manual-only because commission creation grants
    execution authority. MCP lifecycle operations manage claim, preflight,
    start, events, and terminal state after the correct authority boundary.
  </p>

  <p>
    Delivery-policy values are runner-facing governance data. They do not make
    Haft Core apply workspace changes. The external runner must enforce its
    own scope, lockset, and effect-specific authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Separate effects stay separate</h2>

  <p>
    Decision binding, WorkCommission creation, profile application,
    incompatible memory-model selection, rollback, SpecSection approval,
    rebaseline, and reopen are distinct operator effects. Authority for one
    cannot be inferred from another.
  </p>

  <p>
    Automatic singleton profile bootstrap and automatic activation of a proven
    compatible <code>ProjectTypeEnv</code> successor are explicit system
    policies. They are not fabricated operator requests and do not widen any
    other authority.
  </p>

